## Hours logic

Opening hours live in `business.schedule` in `content.js`. The header status,
emergency banner, contact card, and form confirmation all read from it, and the
time is always calculated in Syracuse time, whatever time zone the visitor is in.

## Hero 3D element

A copper supply line with a brass ball valve and pressure gauge sits above the
hero request form; its drop pipe runs behind the form card. Built with
three.js 0.182.0 via React Three Fiber 8.18.0 (both pinned exactly), entirely from procedural geometry. There are
no model or HDR files to download: reflections come from three.js's built-in
`RoomEnvironment`.

- **Interaction:** on desktop the assembly turns slightly toward the cursor.
  On touch screens it sways slowly and tilts a little as the page scrolls, and
  it never captures touches (`touch-action: pan-y`). Clicking or tapping the
  valve turns the lever a quarter turn and the gauge needle drops or rises.
- **Reduced motion:** with `prefers-reduced-motion`, the scene renders on
  demand only: no idle movement, no parallax, and values snap instantly.
- **Performance:** three.js is in its own chunk (about 236 KB gzipped). It is
  only downloaded on capable devices, and only once the browser is idle, so it
  never delays the headline, buttons, or form. Rendering pauses whenever the
  hero is off screen. Pixel ratio is capped at 2 on desktop and 1.5 on touch
  devices, and the canvas is small. Geometry is merged to keep draw calls
  low.
- **Fallback:** the flat SVG version is shown when WebGL is unavailable or
  would run through a slow software renderer (`failIfMajorPerformanceCaveat`),
  when Data Saver is on, or when the device reports under 2 GB of memory. It
  is also shown if the 3D chunk fails to load or the WebGL context is lost.
  It has the same footprint, so the layout never shifts.
- **Testing:** add `?hero3d=off` to the URL to see the fallback, or
  `?hero3d=force` to force 3D (for example, in a browser without a GPU).
- The canvas is decorative (`aria-hidden`), and nothing on the site depends on it.

### Three.js guideline audit

The 3D code was audited against the 53 three.js rules in the ui-ux-pro-max
skill. About 20 don't apply (no GSAP, particles, shadows, glTF models, or
orbit controls), and React Three Fiber covers several more automatically
(render loop, resize via ResizeObserver, raycaster setup, color management).
Fixed in the audit:

- **Exact version pins** instead of `^` ranges.
- **`three/addons/...` import paths** instead of the older `examples/jsm`.
- **Live `prefers-reduced-motion`:** switching it mid-visit freezes or
  resumes the scene immediately.
- **Raycasting limited to the valve:** pipes and gauge opt out.
- **Hover re-checked each frame while the pointer is over the canvas,** so
  the cursor stays correct as the scene eases under a still mouse.
- **Lower-detail geometry for the background pipe run.**

Deliberate deviations:

- **Version.** The guide recommends three 0.185.1, but three.js deprecated
  `THREE.Clock` in r183, and every React Three Fiber release (including 9.x)
  still creates one internally. That would log a deprecation warning for
  every visitor, so the project pins 0.182.0, the last release before the
  deprecation. Move to the newest three when React Three Fiber switches to
  `THREE.Timer`.
- **Camera field of view is 30°** (the guide suggests 45–55° for product
  shots). This keeps the long pipe run from looking stretched and the gauge
  undistorted.
- **`aria-hidden` rather than `role="img"` with a label.** The guide's own
  accessibility guidance marks decorative visuals beside visible text as
  hidden. This scene adds no information and isn't needed for any task.

## Design notes

- **Palette:** ink navy `#0f2438` and deep blue `#163a5f`, with copper `#b85d28`
  (the color of copper pipe) used only for calls to action. Red `#b3271a`
  appears only in the emergency banner so it keeps its urgency.
- **Type:** Barlow Condensed for headlines, Inter for body text.
- **Motion:** one choreographed moment. On load the hero content rises in
  sequence and the 3D valve opens, lifting the gauge needle. Everything else moves only in response to the
  visitor. All motion is disabled for `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- **Accessibility:** skip link, landmarks, visible focus, 44px+ touch targets,
  16px form inputs (no iOS zoom), labeled errors with `aria-invalid`, and an
  accessible FAQ accordion.

